### Date format

The **Date format** field is a list of calendars. Select one to see the dates of the texts converted to that calendar. The available calendars are:
- **Gregorian**: the civil calendar mostly used in the Western part of the world. It was introduced in <htdate0> by Pope Gregory XIII to replace the Julian calendar and correct the drift of the equinoxes. The reform adjusted the leap years and removed ten days from October <htdate1>, and it was adopted gradually in different countries;
- **Hebrew**: the lunisolar calendar of the Jewish tradition. Its months have 29 or 30 days and begin with the new moon. Because twelve lunar months are shorter than a solar year, a thirteenth month (Adar II) is added seven times in every nineteen years so that the festivals stay in their season. It is still used today for Jewish religious life and for the dates of the Hebrew Bible;
- **Hispanic**: the era used in the Iberian Peninsula, counted from <htdate2>. It appears in many documents of the medieval kingdoms of the region and was gradually replaced by the Christian era, so the same event may be dated with two different years in old documents;
- **Islamic**: the lunar calendar of the Islamic tradition. Its twelve months of 29 or 30 days follow the phases of the Moon, and its year is about eleven days shorter than the solar year, so its dates move through the seasons. It begins with the Hijra, the migration of the Prophet Muhammad from Mecca to Medina, in <htdate3>;
- **Julian (Days)**: the Julian Day number, that is, the days counted from 1 January <htdate4>. It was proposed by the scholar Joseph Justus Scaliger and is used by astronomers because it is a single, continuous count of days that does not depend on any calendar reform;
- **Mesoamerican**: the Mayan Long Count, with the Haab and Tzolkin cycles. It was used by the Maya and other peoples of Mesoamerica, combining a 260-day ritual cycle (Tzolkin) with a 365-day solar cycle (Haab). The Long Count measures long spans of time from a starting date in <htdate5>;
- **Mesoamerican (Ext.)**: an extended version of the Mayan Long Count, which adds more places to the count so that much longer periods of time can be written;
- **Persian**: the Solar Hijri calendar used in Iran and Afghanistan. It descends from older Persian calendars and was adopted in its present form in <htdate6>. Its year begins at the March equinox, when the festival of Nowruz is celebrated;
- **French Rev.**: the calendar of the French Revolution. It was adopted in <htdate7>, had twelve months of thirty days and ten-day weeks called décades, and used names inspired by nature and agriculture. Its year began at the autumn equinox;
- **Shaka**: the Saka calendar, the national calendar of India. It is based on the Saka era, which begins in <htdate8>, and was adopted as the national calendar in <htdate9>. Its year also starts near the March equinox;
- **Chinese**: the Chinese lunisolar calendar, showing the stem, the branch, and the animal of the year. It is one of the oldest calendars still in use and combines a cycle of sixty years, formed by ten heavenly stems and twelve earthly branches, with twelve animals. Its lunar months are kept in step with the Sun by adding leap months, and it is still used for festivals such as the Chinese New Year;
- **Aymara**: the calendar of the Aymara people of the Andes. It follows the agricultural and astronomical cycle of the region, and its new year is celebrated at the June solstice with the festival of Willkakuti (the return of the Sun), when the days begin to grow longer again in the southern hemisphere;
- **Mapuche**: the calendar of the Mapuche people of South America. Its year begins at the June solstice with We Tripantu (the new Sun), which marks the renewal of nature, and its months are named after the agricultural and ceremonial activities of each season;
- **Inca**: the calendar of the Inca Empire. It was a solar calendar of twelve months linked to agriculture and to the movement of the Sun, and its year began around the December solstice with the festival of Capac Raymi. The Incas used observatories such as the Intihuatana to follow the Sun;
- **Javanese**: the calendar of Java, in Indonesia. It is a lunisolar calendar created by Sultan Agung of Mataram, who combined the Islamic lunar calendar with the older Hindu-Buddhist Saka calendar of the island. Its months include Sura, Sapar, and Mulud;
- **Japanese**: the Japanese calendar, with the name of the era (nengo). Since <htdate10> it follows the Gregorian months and days, but the years are also counted inside imperial eras, such as Reiwa, which began in <htdate11>. A date may show both the era name and the year.

The same field is useful when comparing documents written in different calendars: choose the same format for all of them.
